steps.body:
Make a folder for season 4 and a file called types.cpp inside it.

Part one, ask the boxes what they can hold.

#include <iostream>
#include <limits>

int main() {
    std::cout << std::numeric_limits<int>::max() << "\n";
    std::cout << std::numeric_limits<long long>::max() << "\n";
    return 0;
}

Compile it and run it. The compile command is g++ -std=c++17 -Wall -o types types.cpp, and then you run the program the folder now contains. Write both numbers into the bug diary before you go on. The first one is the edge of the cliff.

Part two, walk off it. Replace main with this:

int main() {
    int n = 2147483645;
    for (int step = 0; step < 5; step = step + 1) {
        std::cout << n << "\n";
        n = n + 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

Say out loud what the five lines will be before you run it. Then run it. On most machines the count climbs to the number you wrote down, and the next line is a large negative one. Python has never once done that to you.

Now the honest part, and it is the most important sentence of the hour. C++ does not promise you that wrap. Running an int past its top is what the standard calls undefined behaviour, which means the compiler is allowed to do anything at all, and it can do something different once optimisations are switched on. So do not write down what C++ does. Write down what your compiler did today, and write beside it that this is a thing nobody is ever allowed to rely on.

Part three, fix it the right way. Change int n to long long n and run it again. The count sails past the old edge because the house is bigger. Then ask the obvious question out loud: does long long have an edge of its own, and where is it? You printed that number in part one.

Part four, two more promises.

double. Print 0.1 + 0.2 and you will see 0.3, because cout shows six digits unless you ask for more. Now add #include <iomanip> and print std::setprecision(17) before the sum, and look at what is really in the box. Run print(0.1 + 0.2) in Python beside it and compare. Same bits in both, different habits about what to show you.

string. Try to glue a number onto a string with a plus sign: std::string s = "5"; s = s + 3;. That one does not compile, and the message is a Bestiary entry. Then try s += 3; instead, which does compile, and print s.size() afterwards to find out what it quietly did to your string.

Those last two lines are the season in miniature. One of them was refused before it could ever run. The other looked fine, compiled fine, ran fine, and lied to you.

Open the season 1 dice game first, or its rules on paper if the share link is long gone. Read the plan out loud before you write anything: shake, roll two numbers from 1 to 6, the higher number wins, a tie is special.

Write that plan on paper in five lines of plain English, with no language in mind at all. The paper is the thing being translated. The C++ file is only one of its costumes.

One thing genuinely changes, and it goes on the paper: a laptop has no shake sensor, so pressing Enter is the shake.

New file, dice.cpp:

#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <random>

int main() {
    std::mt19937 gen(2027);
    std::uniform_int_distribution<int> die(1, 6);

    std::string one = "Alex";
    std::string two = "Sam";

    std::cout << "Press Enter to roll.\n";
    std::cin.get();

    int rollOne = die(gen);
    int rollTwo = die(gen);

    std::cout << one << " rolled " << rollOne << "\n";
    std::cout << two << " rolled " << rollTwo << "\n";

    if (rollOne > rollTwo) {
        std::cout << one << " wins\n";
    } else if (rollTwo > rollOne) {
        std::cout << two << " wins\n";
    } else {
        std::cout << "A tie. Roll again.\n";
    }
    return 0;
}

Compile it, run it, then run it again. And again.

The same two rolls come out every single time, and that is not a bug. mt19937 is a machine that makes numbers which look random, starting from a number you hand it called a seed, and 2027 is the seed you handed it. Same seed, same numbers, forever. That is a promise rather than a fault, and it is the only reason testing a game with dice in it is possible at all.

To make it roll differently, hand it a seed that changes. Add #include <chrono> and swap the first line of main for these two:

    unsigned seed = (unsigned)std::chrono::steady_clock::now().time_since_epoch().count();
    std::mt19937 gen(seed);

Run it four or five more times. Then say out loud which parts of the season 1 program survived the translation and which did not, and be honest about the last part: this version is not better. It is the same plan in a stricter tongue, and knowing that is the whole point of a Rosetta.
